      <h2>What I Thought</h2>

      <p>
        Before researching these cybersecurity concepts, I thought Zero Trust
        meant that a company had no faith in the software it used. I also
        thought "assume breach" meant that a hole in a company's defenses
        had already happened.
      </p>

      <h2>What I Learned</h2>

      <p>
        Zero Trust is a cybersecurity approach where users, devices, and
        systems are not automatically trusted simply because they are inside
        an organization's network. Access should be verified and limited
        based on factors such as identity, permissions, and device security.
      </p>

      <p>
        Assume Breach means that an organization should operate as though an
        attacker could already be inside its environment. This approach
        encourages organizations to use multiple layers of security, monitor
        activity, limit access, and prepare to detect and respond to attacks.
      </p>

      <div class="contact-box">
        <strong>Key Takeaway</strong>
        <p>
          The biggest gap in my understanding was that these concepts are not
          simply about distrusting software or assuming that a security hole
          has already happened. They are security approaches designed to
          reduce risk by limiting trust, monitoring systems, and preparing
          for possible compromise.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Why This Matters</h2>

      <p>
        These concepts matter because cybersecurity professionals cannot rely
        on a network being completely secure. Security teams need to verify
        access, limit privileges, monitor systems, and prepare for the
        possibility that an attacker could bypass a security defense.
      </p>

      <h2>Connection to My Career Goals</h2>

      <p>
        This connects to my goal of earning a master's degree in cybersecurity
        and pursuing a career in security engineering. Security engineers help
        build and maintain technical defenses, so understanding concepts such
        as Zero Trust and Assume Breach can help me think about cybersecurity
        as multiple layers of protection instead of relying on one defense.
      </p>

      <h2>Reflection</h2>

      <p>
        This brief showed me that cybersecurity terms can sound straightforward
        but have more specific meanings in the field. Going forward, I want to
        focus on understanding the technical meaning behind security concepts
        instead of relying only on the literal meaning of the words.
      </p>

